# Decision Prescriptor: Evolutionary Surrogate-assisted Prescription as an agent network.
#
# The network does not answer "what will happen?" It answers "what should we do,
# and what does each option cost us?" A predictor is fitted from historical data,
# policies are evolved against it, and the result is a Pareto front of strategies
# that no other strategy beats outright. Picking among them is left to a person,
# because that choice encodes what the objectives are worth to them.

{
    # Load the shared LLM configuration from a single source of truth.
    # This allows users to change the model in one file rather than
    # modifying the configuration for each agent network.
    # Note that the file path here is relative to the root level of the repo.
    include "config/llm_config.hocon",

    "metadata": {
        "description": "Multi-agent decision optimizer implementing Evolutionary Surrogate-assisted Prescription. Fits a ridge-regression surrogate from historical records, evolves neural prescriptor policies against it with NSGA-II, and returns a Pareto front of non-dominated strategies rendered as an interactive HTML report with trade-off, parallel-coordinate and convergence charts.",
        "tags": ["decision-optimization", "pareto", "evolutionary", "tool", "visualization"],
        "sample_queries": [
            "Run the built-in delivery staffing example and show me the trade-offs.",
            "What are my options if I want margin above 25% without pushing delivery risk past 0.4?",
            "Which strategy gives the best balance between margin, delivery risk and time to value?",
            "Fit the surrogate with alpha 0.1 and evolve for 80 generations, then write the report.",
        ]
    },

    "max_steps": 40000,
    "max_execution_seconds": 6000,

    "tools": [

        # The front man. Talks to the user and drives the four stages in order.
        # It cannot carry a CodedTool "class" definition, which is why the numeric
        # work all sits in the specialists below.
        {
            "name": "decision_advisor",
            "function": {
                "description": "I turn a decision problem into a set of trade-off options, backed by a model fitted on your own historical data."
            },
            "instructions": """
You are the decision_advisor, the only agent the user talks to. Never mention the
other agents or the tools by name; describe what you are doing in plain language.

Run these four stages strictly in order. Each one depends on the last, so never
skip ahead or run them in parallel.

Stage 1 - load the problem. Call problem_loader. If the user supplied records and
named which fields are context, which are decisions and which are outcomes, pass
them through. If they did not, call it with no arguments to load the built-in
delivery staffing example, and say clearly that the example data is synthetic.

Stage 2 - fit the predictor. Call surrogate_modeler. Report the R-squared per
outcome in your own words. If any outcome scores below 0.5, say plainly that the
model does not explain that outcome well and that the strategies which follow are
indicative only. Do not bury this.

Stage 3 - search for strategies. Call strategy_evolver. It returns the achievable
range of each outcome across the front.

Stage 4 - explain and render. Send the result to tradeoff_explainer for the plain
language reading, then call report_writer to produce the HTML charts. Give the
user the file path.

Rules you must not break:
- Never invent, estimate or fill in a number yourself. Every figure you state must
  have come back from one of the specialists. If you do not have a number, say so.
- Never present one strategy as "the answer". Every strategy on the front is
  optimal in the sense that improving any objective requires giving up another.
  Which trade is acceptable is the user's judgement, not yours.
- If the user asks for the single best option, explain why no such thing exists
  here, then offer the balanced compromise as a starting point for discussion.
- If a stage returns a message starting with "Error:", relay the problem to the
  user in plain language and stop rather than continuing to the next stage.
            """,
            "tools": ["problem_loader", "surrogate_modeler", "strategy_evolver", "tradeoff_explainer", "report_writer"]
        },

        # Stage 1: load records and the field specification into sly_data.
        {
            "name": "problem_loader",
            "function": {
                "description": "Loads historical records and records which fields are context, which are decisions, and which are outcomes to be optimized.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "rows": {
                            "type": "array",
                            "description": "Historical records, each a flat object of field name to number. Omit entirely to load the built-in synthetic example.",
                            "items": {"type": "object"}
                        },
                        "context_fields": {
                            "type": "array",
                            "description": "Fields describing the situation, which cannot be chosen.",
                            "items": {"type": "string"}
                        },
                        "action_fields": {
                            "type": "array",
                            "description": "Fields describing the decision, which can be chosen.",
                            "items": {"type": "string"}
                        },
                        "outcome_fields": {
                            "type": "array",
                            "description": "Fields describing what happened, to be predicted and optimized.",
                            "items": {"type": "string"}
                        },
                        "maximize": {
                            "type": "array",
                            "description": "One boolean per outcome field: true where a larger value is better.",
                            "items": {"type": "boolean"}
                        }
                    },
                    "required": []
                }
            },
            "class": "load_decision_problem.LoadDecisionProblem"
        },

        # Stage 2: fit the predictor that the search will be run against.
        {
            "name": "surrogate_modeler",
            "function": {
                "description": "Fits a ridge-regression surrogate predicting outcomes from context and action, and reports the R-squared per outcome.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "alpha": {
                            "type": "float",
                            "description": "Ridge regularization strength. Larger values trade fit for stability. Defaults to 1.0.",
                            "default": 1.0
                        }
                    },
                    "required": []
                }
            },
            "class": "fit_surrogate.FitSurrogate"
        },

        # Stage 3: evolve prescriptor policies and return the front.
        {
            "name": "strategy_evolver",
            "function": {
                "description": "Evolves neural prescriptor policies against the fitted surrogate using NSGA-II, returning the Pareto front of non-dominated strategies.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "population_size": {
                            "type": "int",
                            "description": "Candidate policies carried between generations, at most 500. Defaults to 60.",
                            "default": 60
                        },
                        "generations": {
                            "type": "int",
                            "description": "Number of generations to run, at most 500. Defaults to 50.",
                            "default": 50
                        },
                        "hidden_dim": {
                            "type": "int",
                            "description": "Width of the prescriptor's hidden layer, at most 32. Defaults to 4.",
                            "default": 4
                        },
                        "seed": {
                            "type": "int",
                            "description": "Random seed; the same seed reproduces a run exactly. Defaults to 0.",
                            "default": 0
                        }
                    },
                    "required": []
                }
            },
            "class": "evolve_prescriptors.EvolvePrescriptors"
        },

        # Reads the numbers the search produced and puts the trade-off into words.
        {
            "name": "tradeoff_explainer",
            "function": {
                "description": "Explains what a Pareto front means in plain language, given the achievable range of each outcome.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "front_summary": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "The JSON summary returned by strategy_evolver."
                        }
                    },
                    "required": ["front_summary"]
                }
            },
            "instructions": """
You are the tradeoff_explainer. You are given the JSON summary of a Pareto front:
the achievable best and worst of each outcome across strategies that no other
strategy beats outright.

Explain, in at most six sentences:
- the span of each outcome, in the units the fields are named in;
- which outcomes are actually in tension, judging only by the ranges you were
  given, and say plainly when you cannot tell from the summary alone;
- what a decision maker is really choosing between.

You must not invent numbers, name a single winner, or recommend a strategy. If the
summary does not support a claim, do not make it. Write for someone who has not
heard of Pareto fronts: say "no option beats every other on everything at once"
rather than leaning on the jargon.
            """
        },

        # Stage 4: render the charts to a self-contained HTML file.
        {
            "name": "report_writer",
            "function": {
                "description": "Renders the Pareto front as a self-contained HTML report with trade-off scatter, parallel-coordinate and convergence charts, plus a data table.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "title": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "Heading for the report page."
                        },
                        "output_path": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "Where to write the HTML file, inside the working directory and ending in .html. Defaults to decision_prescriptor_report.html in the working directory.",
                            "default": "decision_prescriptor_report.html"
                        }
                    },
                    "required": []
                }
            },
            "class": "render_pareto_report.RenderParetoReport"
        }
    ]
}
